diff --git a/Documentation/githooks.adoc b/Documentation/githooks.adoc
index a625e95..d451768 100644
--- a/Documentation/githooks.adoc
+++ b/Documentation/githooks.adoc
@@ -215,6 +215,17 @@ This hook can be used to perform repository validity checks, auto-display
 differences from the previous HEAD if different, or set working dir metadata
 properties.
 
+pre-worktree
+~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+This hook is invoked by linkgit:git-worktree[1] before adding, moving, or removing a linked working tree. A non-zero exit status rejects that operation, including with `--force`. For `prune`, a rejection retains that administrative entry and pruning continues with the remaining entries; no hooks run during a dry run.
+
+The hook receives four arguments: the event (`add`, `move`, or `remove`), the worktree identifier, the old absolute path, and the new absolute path. An inapplicable or unknown path is an empty argument. For `add`, the identifier is empty because the administrative entry has not been allocated yet. For `move` and `remove`, the identifier is the same administrative entry name passed to `post-worktree`.
+
+The hook runs in the invoking repository with the normal hook environment. An `add` rejection happens before creating or resetting a branch, creating the checkout, or replacing a stale registration. A `move` rejection happens before replacing a stale destination registration or moving the checkout. Git can still reject an operation after an accepting hook; acceptance is neither a reservation nor a success notification. Use `post-worktree` to observe completion.
+
+Hooks are not a repository-wide transaction lock. Concurrent commands can both pass a capacity check before either creates its worktree. A count-based hook can enforce a practical limit for sequential requests but does not guarantee an atomic limit. Hooks can be disabled through the normal Git hook configuration.
+
 post-worktree
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
diff --git a/builtin/worktree.c b/builtin/worktree.c
